<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/philosophy-of-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Science works — planes fly, vaccines happen, predictions land — but <em>why</em> it
    works, and what its claims <em>are</em>, is philosophy's business. Philosophy of
    science examines science's own foundations: what separates science from non-science,
    how evidence could ever confirm a general law, whether theories describe reality or
    merely predict it. Far from abstract, these questions settle practical matters —
    what counts as evidence, when to abandon a theory, why replication matters — that
    every research field now runs on. This page builds the four pillars: Hume's problem
    of induction, Popper's demarcation, Kuhn's revolutions, and the realism debate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="demarcation, induction, paradigms, realism" widget="equationStep" config={sciCfg} />

  <h2>The problem of induction</h2>
  <p>
    All scientific generalisation is <strong>induction</strong>: from observed cases to
    unobserved ones — every swan seen is white, therefore all swans are white. David
    Hume (1739) showed the inference has no non-circular justification. Deductive logic
    cannot license it (the conclusion says more than the premises); and any attempt to
    justify induction <em>inductively</em> ("it has worked before") assumes the very
    principle it defends. Every finite set of observations is compatible with infinitely
    many general laws — black swans existed in Australia all along the Europeans were
    "proving" whiteness. Nelson Goodman sharpened the wound (1955): define
    <em>grue</em> as "green before 2100, blue after" — all observed emeralds are grue
    just as truly as they are green, and only an unproven aesthetic preference for
    "green" excludes the grue law. Yet science <em>works</em>. The mature response is
    not to solve Hume but to reframe: science's justification is not certainty but
    <strong>self-correction</strong> — theories are bold conjectures, punished by
    experiment, replaced by better ones. We are not owed induction's truth; we are
    owed a method that fails fast and locally, and science delivers that.
  </p>

  <h2>Demarcation: what makes a theory scientific?</h2>
  <p>
    The <strong>demarcation problem</strong> asks what separates science from
    non-science. The logical positivists' first answer — a claim is meaningful if
    <em>verifiable</em> — collapsed (it failed its own test, and universal laws can't
    be verified by any finite evidence anyway). Karl Popper's replacement is the
    classic: <strong>falsifiability</strong>. A theory is scientific if it
    <em>forbids</em> some observable state of affairs — if it sticks its neck out.
    Astrology and Freudian psychoanalysis, Popper charged, accommodated every outcome
    (forbidding nothing, explaining everything); Einstein's general relativity stuck
    its neck out measurably — starlight should bend by exactly 1.75 arc-seconds near
    the Sun — and Eddington's 1919 eclipse expedition could have destroyed it. The
    asymmetry is the deep point: <em>no number of confirmations proves a universal
    law, but one genuine counter-instance refutes it</em>. Science, on this view, is
    not a pile of proven facts but a population of surviving conjectures.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> falsifiability means scientists spend their days
    trying to disprove their own theories, and that a single failed test kills a
    theory. Neither is true, and Popper didn't think either was. Normal practice is
    <em>confirmatory</em> — testing consequences and extending domains — because
    bold conjecture is the <em>entry</em> condition, not the daily activity; and
    because of Duhem–Quine, apparent refutations are investigated before celebrations
    (the faster-than-light neutrino "anomaly" of 2011 turned out to be a loose fibre-optic
    cable). Falsifiability is a standard for what may <em>enter</em> science, and an
    ideal for how it must remain <em>losable</em> — not a description of anyone's
    working week. The related error: "it's only a theory" — mistaking the everyday
    sense of theory (a hunch) for the scientific one (a tested explanatory structure,
    the most certain thing science offers).
  </p>
  <p>
    Popper's criterion then meets its own hard cases. The <strong>Duhem–Quine
    problem</strong>: experiments never test a theory alone — they test a bundle
    (theory + instruments + auxiliary assumptions + statistics). When Mercury's
    perihelion misbehaved, the blame could fall on Newton's theory or on an undetected
    planet (Vulcan was hunted for decades) — a failed prediction never says
    <em>which</em> conjunct failed, so strict falsification would burn good theories
    regularly. Scientists also legitimately rescue theories with <strong>ad hoc</strong>
    adjustments — adding Neptune saved Uranus's orbit; adding epicycles saved anything.
    Imre Lakatos mediated: science progresses through <strong>research
    programmes</strong> — a hard core (protected from refutation) plus an adjustable
    protective belt, judged <em>progressive</em> if it keeps predicting novel facts
    (Neptune, gravitational lensing) and <em>degenerating</em> if it only explains
    backwards. Demarcation, refined, is less a border fence than a quality
    assessment over time.
  </p>

  <h2>Kuhn: paradigms and revolutions</h2>
  <p>
    Thomas Kuhn's <em>The Structure of Scientific Revolutions</em> (1962) changed the
    discipline by looking at history instead of logic. Mature science operates inside a
    <strong>paradigm</strong> — an exemplar package of theory, methods, instruments and
    standards (Ptolemaic astronomy, Newtonian mechanics, Lavoisier's chemistry).
    <strong>Normal science</strong> is puzzle-solving within the paradigm: the paradigm
    tells you what counts as a puzzle and what a solution looks like. Anomalies —
    stubborn misfits — accumulate at the edges; heroic adjustments absorb them until
    they can't; then <strong>revolution</strong>: a new paradigm replaces the old, and
    the fields' textbooks are rewritten as if it had always been true. Kuhn's
    provocative theses: paradigms are <strong>incommensurable</strong> (they define
    even the shared vocabulary differently — "mass" is not the same concept for Newton
    and Einstein, so no neutral court exists to judge between them), and scientists
    choose paradigms on values (simplicity, fruitfulness) plus faith and sociology, not
    algorithm. The relativist reading ("truth is paradigm-relative") Kuhn explicitly
    rejected — his own claim was historical, not epistemic nihilism: revolutions
    replace worse with better at puzzle-solving, even without a ladder to The Truth.
    The Copernican case illustrates everything: the Ptolemaic system was excellent
    normal science (predictively competitive, mathematically elegant) — its
    replacement took a century of anomalies (retrograde motion epicycles, the calendar)
    plus instruments (the telescope: Venus's phases, Jupiter's moons) plus a new
    exemplar.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Kuhn proved science is irrational or "just
    politics". He showed it is <em>human</em> — community-organised, historically
    contingent, competitive — while retaining real discipline: paradigms fall to
    accumulated anomaly, and the successor must do the old paradigm's successful work
    <em>and</em> more. Read the debates over p-values and climate models today and you
    are watching normal science, anomaly accumulation, and paradigm defence — live.
  </p>

  <h2>Explanation: why, not just what</h2>
  <p>
    Prediction and explanation come apart, and the gap is instructive. Carl Hempel's
    <strong>covering-law model</strong> (1948) said explanation is prediction run
    backwards: derive the phenomenon from laws plus initial conditions. It is elegant,
    and it fails in two telling ways. <strong>Asymmetry</strong>: from the height of a
    flagpole, the sun's angle, and the laws of optics you can <em>derive</em> the
    shadow's length (a genuine explanation) — but from the shadow's length you can
    also derive the flagpole's height, which is not an explanation of it. Logical
    symmetry, explanatory asymmetry. <strong>Irrelevance</strong>: "men who take
    birth-control pills avoid pregnancy" follows from a true law plus a true
    condition, and explains nothing. Something beyond derivation — causation,
    mechanism, unification — must be added, and the modern accounts (causal
    mechanical, unificationist, counterfactual: an explanation tells you how things
    <em>would have differed</em> had the cause differed) each capture a real part.
    The practical stake is current: machine-learning models predict brilliantly and
    explain nothing, which is why "explainable AI" is philosophy of science with a
    funding line.
  </p>

  <h2>Realism versus anti-realism</h2>
  <p>
    When physics says electrons exist, is it describing reality or computing
    predictions? <strong>Scientific realism</strong>: successful theories' unobservables
    are (approximately) real, and the best explanation of science's success —
    instruments, novel predictions, technological miracles — is that it is latching
    onto real structure (the "no miracles" argument). <strong>Anti-realism</strong>
    (instrumentalism, constructive empiricism) treats theories as instruments for
    organising experience: believe the observable consequences, remain agnostic about
    the rest. The strongest anti-realist weapon is the <strong>pessimistic
    meta-induction</strong> (Laudan): history's most successful theories — phlogiston,
    caloric, the luminiferous ether — are now <em>false</em>; by induction, today's will
    fail too, so their entities probably aren't real either. The realist counter
    (Worrall, Kitcher): what survives theory change is <em>structure</em> — Fresnel's
    ether equations survive in modern optics even though the ether didn't — so realism
    should be about structure, not objects. The debate remains open, productive, and
    sharpened by every unobservable that later turns out to be real — atoms, genes,
    neutrinos — and every instrument that turns an "unobservable" into an observed one,
    redrawing the line that both sides were arguing about.
  </p>

  <h2>Science as social knowledge</h2>
  <p>
    One more layer completes the picture: science is not only a logic, it is an
    <em>institution</em>, and Robert Merton (1942) named the norms that make it
    knowledge-producing as a community: <strong>communalism</strong> (results are
    public property — the shared pool that lets replication exist),
    <strong>universalism</strong> (claims are judged by pre-established criteria, not
    by who made them), <strong>disinterestedness</strong> (the reward is credit for
    truth, not payment per conclusion), and <strong>organised scepticism</strong>
    (every claim is up for institutional challenge). The norms are aspirational, not
    descriptions — but their erosion is measurable and consequential: pay-per-result
    incentives and secrecy attack disinterestedness and communalism; celebrity and
    hierarchy attack universalism; publish-or-perish attacks organised scepticism.
    Much of the replication crisis is what Duhem–Quine plus broken Mertonian norms
    looks like in practice. The epistemology of science is, in the end, partly
    <em>design</em>: which social machinery makes fallible humans self-correcting.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Four extensions. <strong>Bayesian confirmation</strong> formalises what Popper's
    logic left vague: evidence E confirms theory T if it raises P(T | E), and competing
    theories are compared by likelihoods — a calculus that explains why one dramatic
    novel prediction (light bending) outweighs a thousand accommodated old facts, and
    why extraordinary claims need extraordinary evidence: a highly improbable
    hypothesis needs an improbably good fit to the data to overcome its prior.
    <strong>Underdetermination</strong>: in practice multiple incompatible theories fit
    all current data (historically, wave versus matrix mechanics were empirically
    equivalent formulations), so values — simplicity, unification, elegance — do real
    choosing; science's objectivity includes, unavoidably, its value judgements.
    <strong>Paul Feyerabend's provocation</strong> ("anything goes"): the history of
    science is too methodologically promiscuous — Galileo's rhetoric, heuristic
    arguments, and dismissed anomalies — for any single method story; the useful
    reading is not that method doesn't matter but that methods evolve <em>with</em>
    the science. And <strong>the replication crisis</strong> is applied philosophy of
    science running right now: psychology's 2015 finding that large fractions of
    landmark results failed direct replication (Open Science Collaboration), the
    p-value and multiple-comparisons debate, publication bias and preregistration
    reforms — each is a demarcation, induction, or Duhem–Quine problem wearing a lab
    coat. The frontier questions: what philosophy of "AI science" (models that
    generate their own hypotheses) should look like, and whether big-data inference —
    prediction without explanation — is science as usual or a new epistemology. Nor
    is demarcation idle: courts, insurers, educators and regulators must decide what
    counts as scientific evidence, and every such ruling is Popper, Kuhn and Duhem–Quine
    applied under deadline. The philosophical literacy this page teaches is not
    decoration on the sciences; it is their maintenance manual.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Popper, The Logic of Scientific Discovery (1959); Kuhn, The Structure of Scientific Revolutions (1962); Hume, A Treatise of Human Nature (1739); Godfrey-Smith, Theory and Reality; Okasha, Philosophy of Science: A Very Short Introduction.</p>
